      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fort Greene takes its name from the 1776 fort built under General Nathanael Greene, a Revolutionary War figure.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Greene%2C_Brooklyn",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The original fort was a star‑shaped earthwork that mounted six 18‑pound cannons, the largest on Long Island.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Greene%2C_Brooklyn",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The neighborhood’s cultural revival in the 1980s and 1990s mirrored the Harlem Renaissance, fueled by artists and Black professionals.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Greene%2C_Brooklyn",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Spike Lee’s 40 Acres & A Mule Filmworks, founded in the mid‑1980s, helped cement Fort Greene’s reputation as a creative hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Greene%2C_Brooklyn",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Walt Whitman branch of the Brooklyn Public Library sits at 93 Saint Edwards Street, serving Fort Greene’s residents.",
